00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:41.840
Is it a simple question? Defining common&nbsp;
nouns is not actually a simple thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:48.800
Humans are terrible at defining common nouns. I'll&nbsp;
give you an example. Define apple without looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.280
it up. All right. Well, I mean, without looking&nbsp;
it up, I guess I'd say, you know, it's a round&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:02:00.560
fruit whose flesh we eat. And sometimes there's&nbsp;
red, yellow or green or orangish sort of skin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:06.320
and often it's crunchy, but sometimes it's&nbsp;
more mealy. And many of them are sweet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:11.680
but some of them are tart. Does that really&nbsp;
help us identify an apple? Not really. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:15.680
all sorts of exceptions. There's many&nbsp;
other fruits which fit that definition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:23.040
So then I can say, well, an apple is now less&nbsp;
domestic. That's the Latin name. So when I say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:28.400
Apple, what I mean is Mollis domestic? That's&nbsp;
just circular. That's not a usable definition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:34.400
I'm not really telling you anything you can use to&nbsp;
